## ABSTRACT
We report the development of metal-organic framework (MOF) based probes for the direct and rapid detection and quantification of perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) by mass spectrometry. Four waterresistant MOFs, ZIF-8, UiO-66, MIL88-A and $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , were coated on poly(dopamine) pre-coated stainless steel needles and used to rapidly preconcentrate PFOA from water for direct analysis by nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry. The analytical performance of each MOF for detecting PFOA was correlated with both the calculated binding energy of the MOF for PFOA and the relative change in the surface area of the MOF upon exposure to PFOA. MOF-functionalized probes can be used for the rapid $(<5\mathrm{\min})$ and sensitive quantification of PFOA molecules at low ng $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ levels in environmental water samples (i.e. tap water, rainwater and seawater) with no sample preparation. The limit of detection of PFOA in ultrapure water was $11.0\ \mathrm{ng\L^{-1}}$ . Comparable accuracy to an accredited analytical method was achieved despite the MOF-functionalized probe approach being ${\sim}40$ times quicker and requiring ${\sim}10$ times less sample. These features indicate that MOF-coated probes are promising for the direct and rapid monitoring of polyfluorinated substances and other pollutants in the field.
## INTRODUCTION
Access to clean water is considered one of the greatest challenges of the coming century owing to the adverse effects of polluted water on human health.1,2 Per- and poly-fluoroalkyl substances, also known as PFASs, are a major class of pollutants of increasing global concern that have become widespread owing to their extended use in a range of common products and industrial applications.2 PFASs are highly resistant to degradation and readily bioaccumulate in the environment.3 One of the most widely known PFASs is perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA), a “key persistent organic pollutant”4 found in water, soil and seafood, with known toxicity in animals.5  Although the toxicological effects of PFOA on humans has not been unambiguously elucidated,5,6  the maximum PFOA levels allowed by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA) in drinking water is $0.56\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\left(1.35{\times}10^{-9}\mathrm{M}\right)$ .7 Consumption of PFOA in drinking water and seafood is known as the major route of exposure,6 and PFOA levels in drinking water and marine ecosystems are monitored by many national regulatory agencies. The detection and quantification of PFOA in environmental samples is primarily performed by using liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS) in accredited analytical laboratories.8 However, the sample preparation and analysis times for LC-MS are long ( $2\textrm{h}$ or longer) and typically involve large sample volumes (up to $1\mathrm{{L}}$ ) as required by the standard EPA method, in which relatively large sample volumes are passed through a solid phase extraction filter to capture the analyte prior LC-MS. The results of analyses can often take several days to weeks to be delivered to authorities.1 Thus, the development of rapid and more sensitive methods for PFAS analysis would enhance efforts to understand PFOA toxicology and improve the monitoring processes for waterways.
There continues to be intense interest in developing and applying mass spectrometry-based methods for the analysis of PFAS in a wide variety of matrices.9,10 One approach in analytical chemistry to decrease sample preparation time and volume is solid-phase microextraction (SPME). In SPME, a sorbent material is used to directly extract and pre-concentrate analytes from the sample.11 Commercial
SPME fibers are usually coated with polymers such as poly(acrylate) and poly(dimethylsiloxane), which are not particularly efficient for extracting PFOA.12 More advanced coatings that allow for a more efficient adsorption of PFAS have been reported that are highly promising, but they can involve the use of chromatography prior to analysis by mass spectrometry13 and relatively long extraction times (up to 1 hour) and relatively large sample volumes (up to 1 L).14 High-performance sorbents for a given analyte using SPME are materials that: i) interact strongly with the target analytes through absorption and/or adsorption; ii) are able to efficiently release the analyte under the extraction conditions; iii) have a high surface area; and iv) have high thermal stability.11 These characteristics can all be met by metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which provide the opportunity to tune their structures, pore sizes and surface chemistries to increase the selectivity for desired molecules of interest. Further, MOFs have mechanical stability, uniform nanoscale cavities (due to their crystalline nature) and high adsorption affinities.11 The functionalization of SPME fibers with MOFs is in its nascent stage.15-20 However, the use of MOFs for the chemical analysis of fluorinated contaminants in water remains unexplored. The incredible potential for combining MOFs with SPME fibers for the analysis PFAS compounds is highlighted by very recent studies that show MOFs can be used for the remediation of PFOA.21,22
The MOF-based SPME probes reported to date have been used almost exclusively to extract volatile and semi-volatile compounds from the headspace above a sample11 prior to relatively long chromatographic separations and mass analysis. As PFOA derivatives typically have exceedingly low vapour pressures, such molecules cannot be readily analyzed by standard gas chromatography-based methods, requiring complex derivatization procedures.23 Thus, LC-MS is the most commonly used method for PFAS analysis. Challenges associated with many MOFs not being resistant to a wide range of solvent and solution conditions and with keeping MOFs attached to the SPME fibers has seen a dearth in MOF-based SPME being developed for sampling liquids to date. The development of a MOF-based SPME probe for liquid samples could however be a solution to mitigating a key issue in mass spectrometry called ‘ion suppression’. In ion suppression, analyte molecules compete for charge with matrix molecules during ionization and thus, significantly reduce analytical performance.24 In addition to LC, another method that was developed in part to reduce ion suppression is nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry (nESI-MS), in which charged droplets with low-micron to sub-micron diameters are sprayed directly into a mass spectrometer at nL $\mathrm{{min^{-1}}}$ sample infusion rates.25,26 Further, MOF-based SPME for liquids would eliminate the use of lengthy LC separations (e.g. $40~\mathrm{min})$ ) in the analysis of PFOA and its derivatives. Here, by leveraging the adsorption characteristics and stability in water of some MOFs, we report the integration of nESI with SPME direct immersion probes for the first time to reduce ion suppression further. The development and extended use of reliable, rapid and sensitive MOF-SPME probes to detect and quantify fluorinated contaminants in environmental water samples without the use of chromatography or sample preparation is reported.
The method developed here to detect PFOA relies on immersing the SPME probe into a water sample. This limits the choice of MOFs that can be used for the coating, since they have to be water resistant. MOF-SPME probes were prepared using four water-resistant MOFs with substantially different degrees of porosity, ZIF-8, UiO-66, MIL-88A and $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , (Figure 1a).18,27,28 The ability of the MOFs to enhance the detection of PFOA was compared and related to their surface areas. PFOA can be extracted onto such MOF-SPME probes from water samples in less than $3~\mathrm{min}$ , and directly inserted into a nESI emitter filled with methanol to desorb and ionize PFOA for detection by MS (Figure 1b).
Figure 1. Diagrams for preparing MOF-coated probes and performing solid-phase microextraction nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry for the direct quantification of PFOA in aquatic environments. (a) The MOF-SPME probes were made by coating a stainless-steel needle with the high adhesive poly(dopamine), followed by the in situ growth of the MOF on the surface. (b) MOF-SPME probes were used to rapidly preconcentrate the PFOA molecules from water samples. The PFOA molecules were then released in ${\sim}10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ methanol in a nESI emitter for analysis by mass spectrometry. (c) The structure of a linear PFOA molecule.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
## Materials and reagents
All solvents and reagents were used as received without further purification. Methanol $(99.8\%$ HPLC grade), perfluorooctanoic acid $(96\%)$ , dopamine hydrochloride, zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(98\%)$ , 2- methylimidazole, zirconium(IV) chloride $(99\%)$ , terephthalic acid $(98\%)$ , terbium(III) chloride hexahydrate $(99.9\%)$ , terephthalic acid disodium salt $(96\%)$ , iron(III) chloride, tris-base, fumaric acid $(>99\%)$ , tube specimen soda glass with poly(ethylene) stopper $75\mathrm{~x~}25\mathrm{~mm}$ ) and perfluorohexanoic acid were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. Dimethylformamide and hydrochloric acid $(32\%)$ were purchased from VWR International Pty Ltd. Nitric acid $(69\%)$ was purchased from Merck. Acupuncture needles (o.d. $0.22\mathrm{mm}$ , length $40\mathrm{mm}$ ; Sensi) were purchased from Acuneeds (Melbourne, VIC, Australia). Glass capillaries $(\mathrm{o.d.}=1.2\ \mathrm{mm})$ were purchased from Harvard Apparatus (USA). Mechanical stirrer with 6 stirring places and magnetic stirrer bars were used. All aqueous solutions were prepared using high purity Milli-Q water. Stock solution of PFOA was prepared at $10\mathrm{mM}$ in HPLC-grade methanol and stored at 4 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Different concentrations of the PFOA solutions were prepared fresh on the day of the experiment by diluting the stock solution with matrices (ultrapure water or seawater) to given concentrations. The environmental water samples were collected following the EPA Method 537 and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for up to 6 months. Tap water was collected from a kitchen tap in the School of Chemistry, UNSW, Sydney, rain water samples were collected from a paddle on the ground after the rain in Kingsford (NSW, Australia) and seawater samples were collected from Ramsgate beach and Claydon reserve in Botany Bay (NSW, Australia), which was reported as a PFOA contamination site.29 Seawater with minimal contamination was collected from the Ku-ring-gai Chase National Park (NSW, Australia).
## Preparation of pure MOFs particles
ZIF-8 particles were prepared by mixing two separate solutions; $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(12.5~\mathrm{mM})$ in methanol ( $7.5\mathrm{mL}$ ) and 2-methylimidazole $(25\mathrm{mM})$ ) in methanol $7.5\mathrm{mL}$ ).30 The solution was stirred (300 rpm) and allowed to react at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was washed 3 times with methanol, collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum overnight. UiO-66 particles were prepared by mixing two separate solutions; $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ in ethanol $(0.04\mathrm{mM},5.6\mathrm{mL})$ ) and another solution of terephthalic acid (25 mM) and acetic acid $\mathrm{(0.24~mM)}$ in dimethylformamide $(9.4~\mathrm{mL}$ ).30 The reaction was stirred $(300\mathrm{rpm})$ at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was washed 3 times with ethanol, collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum overnight. MIL-88A particles were prepared by mixing two separate solutions; $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $(0.2\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5\mathrm{mL})$ ) and fumaric acid $(0.2\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5\mathrm{mL}$ ).31 The solution was stirred $(300\mathrm{rpm})$ ) and allowed to react at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was washed 3 times with water, collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum overnight. $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ particles were prepared by two separate solutions; $\mathrm{TbCl}_{3}$ $(10\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5\mathrm{mL})$ ) and terephthalic acid disodium salt ( $\mathrm{i}0\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5\mathrm{mL}$ ).32 The solution was stirred $(300~\mathrm{rpm})$ and allowed to react at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was washed with water 3 times, collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum overnight.
## Preparation of poly(dopamine) coated needles
The stainless steel needles were scratched with P1000 sand paper to increase the surface roughness, washed with ultrapure water and dried in air. The deposition of a poly(dopamine) (PDA) layer on the needles (about $2.5\mathrm{cm}$ in length) was conducted in a freshly prepared dopamine solution (2 mg mL1) in Tris-HCl buffer (10 mM, $\mathrm{pH}=8.5$ ), under stirring $(300~\mathrm{rpm})$ at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .33 The resulting PDA coating was then dried with $\Nu_{2}$ . The PDA deposition process was repeated twice to obtain a uniform layer of PDA.
## Preparation of MOF-SPME probes
All the MOF-SPME probes were prepared by immersing the PDA coated needles into a solution containing MOF precursors (metal ions and organic linkers) under mild stirring. The needles were kept at a defined height into the solution using a custom-made holder, as illustrated in Figure S1. Such a holder was used to coat the needles lengthwise by $2.5\pm0.2$ cm with MOFs. The preparation of ZIF-8 coated probes were adapted from ref. 30. The PDA coated needles were immersed in a glass tube containing a solution freshly prepared by mixing $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(12.5~\mathrm{\mM})$ in methanol $7.5~\mathrm{\mL})$ and 2- methylimidazole $(25\mathrm{mM})$ in methanol $7.5\mathrm{mL}$ ). The solution was stirred $(300\mathrm{rpm})$ at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ to obtain ZIF-8 coated SPME probes, which were washed with methanol and dried under vacuum overnight. The preparation of UiO-66 coated probes were adapted from ref.30. The PDA coated needles were immersed in a glass tube containing a solution freshly prepared by mixing $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ in ethanol (0.04 mM, 5.6 mL) and a solution of terephthalic acid $(25~\mathrm{\mM})$ and acetic acid $(0.24~\mathrm{\mM})$ in dimethylformamide $(9.4~\mathrm{mL})$ ). The solution was stirred $(300~\mathrm{rpm})$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain UiO-66 coated SPME probes, which were washed with ethanol and dried under vacuum overnight. The preparation of MIL-88A (Fe) coated probes were adapted from ref.31. The PDA coated needles were immersed in a glass tube containing a solution freshly prepared by mixing $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $(0.2\mathrm{mM})$ in water (7.5 mL) and fumaric acid $(0.2\mathrm{mM})$ in water ( $7.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ). The solution was stirred $(300\mathrm{rpm})$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain MIL-88A coated SPME probes, which were washed with water and dried under vacuum overnight. The preparation of $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ coated probes were adapted from ref.32. The PDA coated needles were immersed in a glass tube containing a solution freshly prepared by mixing $\mathrm{Tb}{\mathrm{Cl}_{3}}$ $\mathrm{i}0\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5~\mathrm{mL})$ and terephthalic acid disodium salt $(10~\mathrm{mM})$ in water $(7.5~\mathrm{mL})$ . The solution was stirred (300 rpm) at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ coated SPME probes, which were washed with water and dried under vacuum overnight.
## Mass spectrometry
Mass spectra were acquired on a linear quadrupole ion trap mass spectrometer (LTQ XL, Thermo Scientific, San Jose, USA) that is equipped with an electrodynamic ion funnel (Heartland Mobility, KS, USA) and a custom nanoelectrospray ionization source. MOF-SPME probes with three ZIF-8 coatings were immersed with some mild agitation into $5\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous sample of interest for 3 min to extract PFOA molecules onto the probe. The probe was then inserted into a borosilicate nanoelectrospray ionization capillary (internal tip diameter of ${\sim}500~\mathrm{nm})^{34}$ containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of methanol. A potential of ${\sim}1.5\mathrm{kV}$ was applied to the SPME probe relative to the capillary entrance to the mass spectrometer to initiate and maintain electrospray ionization. To obtain a calibration curve, perfluorohexanoic acid (PFHA; $m/z=314$ ) was used as an internal standard at a concentration of $40{\times}10^{-15}\mathrm{M}$ . The ion abundances for PFOA and PFHA were averaged from 0 to $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Each solution was run in triplicate and seven different concentrations were analyzed to obtain a linear standard curve. To account for matrix effects, the quantification of PFOA from seawater samples was performed using the well-established standard addition method, in which the environmental samples were spiked with known concentrations of PFOA $(\mathrm{from}8.28\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ L^{-1}$ to $8.28~\mathrm{{ng}~L^{-1}}$ , corresponding to $2{\times}10^{-11}\mathrm{{M}}$ to $2{\times}10^{-8}\mathrm{M}$ ) into $5\mathrm{mL}$ polypropylene vials and run in triplicate.
## DFT calculations
Static binding energies for PFOA in ZIF-8, UiO-66 and MIL-88A frameworks were calculated using density functional theory (DFT). The effect of solvent (water) on the binding energy of PFOA in
all the three structures were studied using the implicit solvent model as implemented in the software VASPsol.35,36 See SI for more details on the simulation methodology. Static binding energies $(\Delta\mathrm{E})$ at $0\mathrm{K}$ in vacuum and in solvent (water) were calculated using equation (1):
$$
\Delta E=E_{M O F+P F O A}-E_{M O F}-E_{P F O A}
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{x}}$ refers, respectively, to the total energies of the $\mathrm{MOF}+\mathrm{PFO}.$ A complex, the MOF alone, and PFOA molecule. Table S1 shows the calculated binding energies of PFOA in all three structures in water using the implicit solvent model.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
MOF-SPME probes were prepared by coating stainless steel needles with a PDA layer, followed by MOF nucleation and growth on the PDA coated needles (Figure 1a).  The PDA layer was used to improve the adhesion and nucleation of MOFs crystals.30 The resultant probes were stored under vacuum prior to subsequent experiments. In order to understand the analytical performance of using different MOF coatings and to confirm the expected MOFs were synthesized under these conditions, the coatings were thoroughly characterized by solid state and surface analysis methods. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping confirmed the formation of a uniform MOF coating around the surface of the needle (Figure 2a-b and Figures S2-S8). From the cross sections of the needles, the thickness of the coatings were estimated to be ca. 2.7, 17.1, 4.4, and $7.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for the $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , UiO-66, MIL-88A, and ZIF-8 coatings, respectively (Figure 2c and Figure S9). Small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) patterns collected from the MOF powder and the MOF-coated SPME probes showed peak positions and relative intensities that were in agreement with the SAXS patterns for MOF powders made in-house,37-40 further confirming that MOF crystals were successfully attached on the surfaces of the microneedles (Figure 2d and Figure S10). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns collected from such MOF powders matched well with the patterns reported in literature (Figure S11),41- 43 suggesting that the crystals formed on the probes correspond to UiO-66, ZIF-8, MIL-88A and $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ . The surface areas of the four MOFs as measured by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis were within an average of $14\%$ of the corresponding values reported in the literature (Table S2 and Figure S12).43-47
For MOF-SPME nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry, three key steps are followed: i) the MOF-SPME is immersed into a water sample and PFOA molecules are adsorbed onto the surface of the probe; ii) the probe surface is submerged into a small volume of organic solvent $(<10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ to desorb PFOA; and iii) the resulting solution is nanoelectrosprayed directly into a mass spectrometer to detect
PFOA. In order to compare the analytical sensitivity of the different MOFs coatings, the probes were immersed into an aqueous solution containing $8.28\mathrm{mgL^{-1}}$ PFOA and the recorded ion abundances at $m/z$ $=413$ (corresponding to deprotonated PFOA) were divided by the total weighed mass of each surface coating, which was obtained via ICP-MS analysis. The ion abundances normalized for coating masses are shown in Figure 3, and the raw data are shown in Table S3 (ICP), Figure S13 and Table S4 (ion abundances). The thickness of the coatings obtained via SEM correlated almost linearly with the mass obtained via ICP (Figure S14). $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ generated the lowest signal per unit of mass of the four coatings, followed by MIL-88A which was about 5 times more intense. The signal per unit of mass from ZIF-8 and UiO-66 were about twice as intense as that for MIL-88A, and comparable to each other. However, the use of UiO-66 was less reproducible compared to ZIF-8, mostly due to the variability in the MS results. The ion abundance for PFOA obtained using each MOF was linearly correlated with the BET surface areas of the MOFs upon exposure to PFOA; that is, higher BET surface area corresponded to higher PFOA ion abundance.
To investigate the interactions of PFOA with the MOF coatings, DFT calculations were performed assuming that the PFOA molecule is adsorbed within the pores of MOF except for $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ which does not have sufficiently large pores to internally accommodate PFOA molecules; that is, $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ can be used to investigate the impact of MOF porosity on the efficiency of PFOA adsorption. To include the effect of solvent on the interaction energy, an implicit solvation model for water is considered in the calculations (see SI for further simulation details). The DFT calculations (Table S1) suggested that ZIF8 and UiO-66 have similar binding energies for PFOA, which are significantly higher than those for MIL88A. This qualitative trend for the binding energies is in agreement with the analytical sensitivity of the different MOFs coatings shown in Figure 3; that is, MOFs with higher calculated binding energies tend to result in higher performance for detecting PFOA. The BET surface area of the material was measured before and after exposing ${\sim}60~\mathrm{mg}$ of MOF powder to a $4.14\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ ( $10~\mathrm{mM})$ solution of PFOA in water (PFOA solubility in water is $9.5\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , or $23\ \mathrm{mM^{48}}$ ) for $3~\mathrm{min}$ . The BET results (Table S5) showed that the surface area for the four MOFs after introducing PFOA molecules decreased by approximately $25\%$ for ZIF-8, $28\%$ for UiO-66, $9\%$ for MIL-88A and $7\%$ for $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ . The large decrease in surface area for ZIF-8 and UiO-66 is consistent with the PFOA molecules being primarily adsorbed inside the pores of these MOFs. However, in the case of MIL-88A and $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ a very small change is observed in BET surface area and thus, for these two MOFs, the PFOA is primarily adsorbed on the surface of the MOFs or between the particles, rather than in the pores.
Figure 3. Correlation between the analytical results for the detection of PFOA at the concentration of $8.28~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ by MOF-SPME probes and the surface area of the MOF. The PFOA ion abundance was obtained by MOF-SPME nESI-MS for four different MOFs coatings, divided by the total weighed mass of each coating. The error bars were obtained by propagation of the uncertainty in the ion abundances (standard deviation of the MS results for three samples) and in the mass of the coatings (standard deviation of the ICP results for three samples). The surface areas were obtained from BET measurements.
Overall, both the PFOA binding energies and the decrease in the surface area upon exposure to PFOA were in agreement with the experimental trend in the performance of the different surface coatings in MOF-SPME nESI-MS for the detection of PFOA. That is, the performance of MOFs in detecting PFOA may potentially be predicted by calculating binding energies and measuring the decrease of surface area of MOF powders upon exposure to PFOA. Such an approach may be useful for screening different types of MOFs before preparing and testing new MOF-SPME probes. Of the MOFs tested in this study, the use of ZIF-8 and UiO-66 resulted in similar performance, calculated binding energies and decreases in surface area upon exposure to PFOA. However, ZIF-8 was chosen for the quantification of PFOA in environmental samples because of the higher reproducibility in the detection of PFOA and lower cost than UiO-66. Moreover, ZIF-8 can be readily prepared at room temperature unlike UiO-66.
The ability to detect PFOA requires the MOF to have an affinity for the fluorinated molecule in water (in order to preconcentrate the pollutant), but also to release the analyte during the MS measurement. To test the desorption process, the PFOA-treated ZIF-8 powder was washed with methanol and measured via BET. It was observed that the surface area $(2,685\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) increased to a value slightly higher than the value $(2,410\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of ZIF-8 powder prior to absorbing the PFOA molecules, which indicates that methanol was effective in eluting the PFOA molecules after the pre-concentration process. This slight increase in surface area could be explained by the introduction of defect sites as a result of the PFOA treatment.22,49 ZIF-8 is stable in aqueous solutions at pH values above 7.50  The pH of the sample solutions containing the MOF powders and PFOA was a value of 8.4; i.e. the addition of PFOA does not lower the pH of the solution to any significant extent and is unlikely to cause decomposition of the ZIF-8 material owing to acidity. The long-range order of the crystal was retained after methanol washing, as shown by the powder XRD and in-situ SAXS patterns in Figure S15. In addition, any MOF degradation during the desorption process was not detected by mass spectrometry and is not considered an issue, since these probes were designed for single use.
The preparation of the ZIF-8 coated probe and the detection process were optimized using a solution containing $8.28~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $(2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{M})$ of PFOA in ultrapure water (18 MΩ cm). The first parameter investigated was the contact time between the probe and the PFOA solution. It was found that the ion abundance increased with the immersion time (Figure S16) and reached a plateau at 200 to 300 s. Immersion times longer than 300 s were not considered as the goal was to develop a rapid sampling and analysis method.
To obtain a reproducible ZIF-8 layer on the needle and thus, a reproducible MS signal for PFOA, coating conditions were important. The optimal mixing speed for the MOF precursors was $300~\mathrm{rpm}$ (Figure S17), while the preferred surface treatment was to grow a layer of PDA on scratched needles (Figure S18). The uniformity of the PDA layer was checked at the optical microscope (Figure S19). Repeating the ZIF-8 coating process sequentially on the same needle resulted in the PFOA ion abundances increasing significantly between 0 and 3 layers and then plateauing with further coatings; i.e. three coatings can be considered an optimal compromise between signal and the time required to prepare the probes (Figure S20). Under these conditions, the MOF formed a uniform coating of about $9.8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ around the needles (Figure S21); these probes were made using three sequential ZIF-8 coating steps and were used in all subsequent experiments. Regarding the storage method for the probes, it was found that storing the ZIF-8 SMPE probes under vacuum until immediately before the MS measurement minimized the variability in the measured PFOA ion abundances compared to storing them in air or under nitrogen (Figure S22). Such an observation can be attributed to volatile contaminants being trapped in the MOF and potentially reducing the available pores for capturing PFOA.
The coating on the SPME probe should efficiently adsorb PFOA from water, and also release the fluorinated compound once the probe is immersed in an appropriate solvent. Such a solvent should efficiently solvate PFOA and be compatible with nanoelectrospray ionization. For the solutions tested (methanol, $1\%$ acetic acid in methanol, $1\%$ sodium hydroxide in methanol, $1\%$ triethylamine in methanol, $1\%$ triethylamine in acetonitrile and ethyl acetate) that are compatible with nanoelectrospray ionization for the detection of negative ions and the MOFs of interest, the use of methanol resulted in the highest performance in terms of signal intensity and reproducibility (Table S6). This is in agreement with a previous report that suggests PFOA is preferentially solvated by methanol molecules in water-methanol solutions,51 which facilitates the desorption process with ${\sim}98\%$ of the adsorbed PFOA molecules that can be detected being rapidly released within 5 min (Figure S23). In addition, methanol has a high vapour pressure and can thus, readily desolvate during the electrospray ionization process to improve the measured ion abundance compared to the use of water. Under these optimized conditions, the intra-batch reproducibility was between $\pm2.5$ to $\pm11\%$ , while the inter-batch reproducibility was $\pm16\%$ . These were quantified by using three probes from three different batches to measure a solution of $8.28~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ PFOA in seawater.
Representative mass spectra obtained by using a ZIF-8 SPME probe dipped in ultrapure water in the absence and in presence of PFOA are shown in Figure 4a. While no PFOA signal was detected for blank ultrapure water, two abundant peaks appeared from the PFOA-spiked solution that corresponded to deprotonated PFOA $(m/z413)$ and decarboxylated PFOA $(m/z~369)$ were observed. The latter fragment ion arises from the loss of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from deprotonated PFOA in the ion source and can be used as a diagnostic fragment ion for PFOA. Owing to the selectivity of MS, PFOA can be readily distinguished from other pollutants present in environmental water samples. Similar spectra of seawater samples with endogenous and spiked PFOA are shown in Figure S24. This latter result is particularly important as the ability to perform MS analysis from samples obtained from seawater demonstrates the ability of the ZIF-8 SPME to mitigate the impact of ion suppression.
The analytical performance of the ZIF-8 SPME probe was further investigated by obtaining a calibration curve for PFOA spiked in ultrapure water and seawater (Figure 5 and Table S7). In ultrapure water, the sensitivity was $2.43{\times}10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ and the limit of detection (LOD) was $11.0\ \mathrm{ng\L^{-1}}$ $(2.67{\times}10^{-11}\mathrm{~M}_{;}$ , see Supporting Information for the calculations). This LOD was nearly a factor of two lower than the limit of reporting for the LC-MS method $(20\ \mathrm{ng\L^{-1}}$ , or $4.83\times10^{-11}\mathrm{~M})$ employed in an accredited laboratory (National Measurement Institute, North Ryde, NSW, Australia),52,53 which indicates that ZIF-8 SPME could be used to detect PFOA at concentrations that are well below the USEPA safe drinking water limits. The sensitivity in seawater $(7.75{\times}10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{-1})$ ) was higher than in ultrapure water $(2.43\times10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{\mathrm{-1}})$ ), which may be related to matrix effects. The same increase in signal was observed when comparing an uncontaminated seawater sample and ultrapure water, both spiked with $8.28~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ PFOA. A possible explanation is that the alkalinity of seawater ( $\mathrm{\pH}8.2-8.3$ for both samples) compared to ultrapure water (near $\mathrm{pH}7$ ) should facilitate deprotonation of PFOA during the ESI process. However, other species present in the seawater could also affect the ion abundance, in addition to the adsorption and desorption efficiencies. The adsorption of PFOA onto the ZIF-8 SPME probe follows a Langmuir isotherm (Figure S25), meaning that the surface is homogenous and contains single sites for adsorption as reported for the adsorption of PFOA onto other MOFs.22 The advantage of the MOF based SPME approach compared to alternative SPME methods reported in the literature for PFOA is that the probe immersion time can be significantly shortened (3 min compared to $60\mathrm{min}$ ) and lower samples volumes can be used (5 mL compared to $1\mathrm{L}$ ), while achieving a LOD that is comparable to previous reports in the low ng/L range.14,54,55
Next, this method was used to examine environmental samples that are known to be contaminated with PFOA. In Figure 4b, the signal-to-noise ratios for PFOA are reported for contaminated samples of tap water, rainwater and two seawater samples from two locations. It was found that the ion abundance of PFOA in the environmental samples was a respective 7.6, 14.9, 66.0 and 25.7 times higher than the background signal measured in ultrapure water (blank), which indicates that PFOA can be detected in all samples. The abundance of PFOA in tap water and rainwater were significantly lower than in the two seawater samples, and the seawater from Ramsgate was heavily contaminated. The assignment of the peaks at $m/z\ 369$ and 413 to PFOA was further confirmed by spiking the water samples with a standard PFOA solution, which resulted in an increase in the abundances of these peaks (Figure S24).
the ZIF-8 SPME probes. (a) ZIF-8 SPME nESI mass spectra of $8.28~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $(20~\mathrm{nM})$ PFOA in ultrapure water (upper blue trace) and blank ultrapure water (lower red trace). The peaks at $m/z~413$ and 369 correspond to deprotonated PFOA and a diagnostic fragment ion for PFOA. (b) Relative abundance for the detection of PFOA in environmental samples measured by ZIF-8 SPME nESI mass spectrometry. The relative abundance for PFOA ions measured by mass spectrometer were obtained from the average integrated deprotonated ion signal at $m/z413$ divided by the average integrated abundance at this same $m/z$ in a blank ultrapure water sample. (c) PFOA quantification using the ZIF-8 SPME probes. Standard addition curve for PFOA spiked into a seawater sample (circle dots) and a calibration curve for PFOA in blank ultrapure water (triangle dots) obtained using ZIF-8 SPME nESI-MS. The relative PFOA abundance was calculated by dividing the ion signal at $m/z$ 413 by the ion signal at $m/z$ 314 (internal standard). Error bars correspond to $\pm1$ standard deviation for three replicates.
The concentration of PFOA in environmental samples affects the type and scope of intervention that is required. Thus, quantification of PFOA in environmental water samples is important in monitoring water pollution. The concentration of PFOA in a seawater sample from Ramsgate beach was performed via the standard addition method (refer to Supporting Information for full details). By use of this method, a concentration for PFOA of $117.6\pm7.2~\mathrm{ng~L^{-1}}$ ( $(2.84\pm1.75){\times}10^{-10}\mathrm{~M})$ was obtained for seawater collected from Ramsgate beach (Figure 4). The same seawater sample was also analyzed by the accredited laboratory in the National Measurement Institute via a standard LC-MS method (Table S8). The concentration of PFOA was found to be $149.9\pm7.5\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , which was within $20\%$ that obtained by using the ZIF-8 SPME approach.
## CONCLUSIONS
This study reports a MOF-based probe for the fast and sensitive detection of PFOA in several environmental water samples for direct and rapid analysis using nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry. Among the four water-resistant MOFs tested (ZIF-8, UiO-66, MIL88-A and $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3})$ ), the use of ZIF-8 coated SPME probes resulted in significant performance gains in terms of sensitivity per unit mass of the coating and reproducibility. The performance of the various MOF-SPME probes correlated with the strengths of the calculated PFOA binding energies in water, and the measured decrease in the MOF surface area when exposed to aqueous solutions containing PFOA. Such calculations and measurements could be used to identify additional MOFs for the detection of other environmental contaminants by SPME nanoelectrospray ionization mass spectrometry. The optimized ZIF-8 SPME probe can be used for the direct detection of PFOA in environmental samples (i.e. tap water, rainwater and seawater) within 5 min without any sample preparation or chromatography at trace levels $\mathrm{11.0~ng}$ $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ limit of detection) in low sample volumes $\mathrm{\Delta}5\mathrm{mL}$ ). As a comparison, the conventional LC-MS approach (e.g. EPA Method 537) requires over $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ per sample and $1\mathrm{~L~}$ of sample. By use of MOF-SPME nanoelectrospray ionization MS, the PFOA concentration in a contaminated seawater sample was quantified $(117.6\pm7.2\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and was in agreement with the value obtained from a certified laboratory using the EPA 537 LC-MS method. Overall, these results, combined with low amount of coating material needed for detection $(<30~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ , indicate that MOF-functionalized SPME probes are very well suited to be implemented in a portable ‘kit’ for the on-site monitoring of polyfluoroalkyl substances and other environmental pollutants. The MOF-coated probes have the advantage that the materials required for their fabrication are inexpensive $(<\$2$ USD/probe) and can be considered disposable, which is useful for eliminating biofouling issues associated reusable SPME probes for sampling environmental water samples.56-58 Thus, the MOF-coated probes developed here have the potential to be deployed on a largescale to efficiently collect hundreds to thousands of samples in parallel for rapid PFAS analysis in environmental water samples. We envision the development of advanced probes for real-time and accurate detection on site will be useful for the rapid and sensitive monitoring of polyfluoroalkyl substances and other contaminants in the environment to ensure water quality.
